╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ steiner_relay_broadcast                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of computing independent per-destination Dijkstra shortest paths (which           │
│                          │ duplicates edges per partition and ignores shared structure), this approach builds a      │
│                          │ shared broadcast relay tree (Steiner-tree style): it finds a low-cost tree rooted at src  │
│                          │ that reaches all destinations, so common prefixes of paths are reused, dramatically       │
│                          │ reducing redundant edge transfers. Each partition still gets a full path per destination  │
│                          │ (traversing tree edges), but shared upstream edges only count once in overall transfer.   │
│                          │ Additionally, partitions are diversified: each partition's route to a destination is      │
│                          │ chosen from the k-shortest simple paths to balance load across parallel network links and │
│                          │ avoid bottlenecking a single expensive path. The algorithm: (1) compute k-shortest paths  │
│                          │ per dst by cost; (2) greedily select paths that maximize overlap with already-chosen      │
│                          │ edges (prefix sharing) while keeping edge usage bounded (load balancing); (3) emit the    │
│                          │ resulting paths per (dst, partition). This reduces total cost versus naive per-partition  │
│                          │ Dijkstra replication.                                                                     │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
