╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ multi_partition_path_diversity_split_copy_multicast_tree                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The current algorithm sends every partition of data from src to each destination along    │
│                          │ the *same* single shortest path. This multiplies the cost by num_partitions and congests  │
│                          │ one route while parallel alternative networks sit idle. A better strategy: (1) only send  │
│                          │ data once over shared links — build a multicast Steiner-tree style routing where common   │
│                          │ prefixes of paths to multiple destinations are traversed only once, and (2) distribute    │
│                          │ partitions over edge-disjoint / k-shortest paths so that different partitions use         │
│                          │ different (possibly parallel) network routes, avoiding redundant transfers and balancing  │
│                          │ load. Since the BroadCastTopology records per-partition per-destination edge lists, we    │
│                          │ can assign partition i of each dst a path chosen from the k-shortest simple paths (by     │
│                          │ cost), cycling among them so no single path carries all partitions. For shared subtrees,  │
│                          │ we approximate by removing src in-edges and using k-shortest paths per dst. This reduces  │
│                          │ total cost when alternative cheap paths exist and lowers congestion.                      │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0049                                                                                   │
