│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ congestion_aware_multicast_search                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of ranking k candidate paths once and round-robining partitions, this approach    │
│                          │ treats broadcast routing as an iterative congestion-aware assignment problem. It          │
│                          │ generates a large pool of candidate paths per destination using cost-weighted randomized  │
│                          │ edge sampling (a Monte-Carlo / ant-colony style exploration around the cheapest routes),  │
│                          │ then assigns each partition greedily by marginal cost: path_cost + λ·(current_edge_load / │
│                          │ throughput). This lets cheap shared trunk edges absorb most partitions while lightly      │
│                          │ penalizing congestion, and expensive detours only get used when the cheap route is        │
│                          │ saturated. Load and cost are jointly optimized instead of being sequentially ranked,      │
│                          │ which should lower combined cost while keeping transfers overlappable across parallel     │
│                          │ links.                                                                                    │
