├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ shared_trunk_multicast_steiner                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of computing an independent shortest path per destination (which duplicates       │
│                          │ transfer cost when destinations share route segments), this approach builds a directed    │
│                          │ Steiner-tree-like multicast structure: it models the problem as a minimum-cost            │
│                          │ arborescence rooted at src spanning {src} ∪ dsts (approximated with a prune-based MST     │
│                          │ over the metric closure / or repeated shortest-path grafting). All destinations share     │
│                          │ overlapping trunk edges, so cost is paid once per shared edge rather than once per        │
│                          │ destination. Additionally, for edges with very low throughput relative to demand, the     │
│                          │ algorithm splits a partition's traffic across edge-disjoint parallel paths to balance     │
│                          │ load and avoid bottleneck costs, and it prunes the final tree of destination-unreachable  │
│                          │ branches. This should substantially reduce total cost versus per-destination Dijkstra     │
│                          │ when destinations cluster behind common gateways (typical in multi-cloud topologies where │
│                          │ several regions connect through the same backbone hubs).                                  │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
