│ patch_name               │ seq_discount_greedy_order                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ Best known approach is the sequential Steiner-like discount framework (Gen 10: cost 756): │
│                          │ process destinations one at a time, and once an edge is paid for by an earlier            │
│                          │ destination, subsequent destinations see it as nearly free (cost 0.001), encouraging      │
│                          │ shared-tree transfers and eliminating redundant parallel transfers. Key parameters to     │
│                          │ improve: (1) destination ordering — I replace static ascending-cost ordering with greedy  │
│                          │ dynamic ordering: at each step, choose the destination whose discounted path cost gives   │
│                          │ the largest savings relative to its original shortest-path cost (maximizing immediate     │
│                          │ edge reuse), a classic greedy Steiner-tree heuristic; (2) discount magnitude 0.001        │
│                          │ (keep); (3) capacity fallback — no throughput penalty since it hurt in other variants.    │
│                          │ Unreachable destinations are handled gracefully with empty partition paths. Per-edge      │
│                          │ append format `[s, t, G]` for all partitions kept exactly as in the proven framework.     │
