│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ steiner_discounted_dijkstra                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ Key parameters from the current program: k_paths=4 candidate enumeration, a reuse "bonus" │
│                          │ divisor, and expensive-first destination ordering. The current reuse-bonus scoring is a   │
│                          │ weak proxy for shared-cost savings (it only discounts by a factor, not by actual edge     │
│                          │ cost). The new algorithm replaces it with true discounted-edge Dijkstra (used edges cost  │
│                          │ ~0 while undiscovered edges keep full $/GB cost), which is a Kou–Markowsky-style          │
│                          │ Steiner-tree heuristic. Destinations are processed in a greedy dynamic order (largest     │
│                          │ immediate savings locked in first), each destination's path is appended to all            │
│                          │ partitions, and used edges are marked so later branches ride the already-paid trunk.      │
│                          │ Unreachable destinations get empty partitions. This directly minimizes the sum of         │
│                          │ unique-edge costs (the observed cost metric) instead of approximating it with path-count  │
│                          │ bonuses.                                                                                  │
