╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ swap_local_search_postpass                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The current sequential greedy k-path assignment suffers from lock-in: the first           │
│                          │ destinations pick cheap paths, and later ones may fail to find better shared routes. Add  │
│                          │ a local-search improvement pass after the initial assignment. For each destination,       │
│                          │ recompute a shortest path on a graph where edges already used by *other* destinations are │
│                          │ discounted (since shared edges are effectively amortized across the broadcast). If        │
│                          │ routing all of this destination's partitions along the new set of edges yields a lower    │
│                          │ total unique-edge cost than its current assignment, keep the swap; otherwise revert. This │
│                          │ exploits multi-network redundancy (discounting shared edges steers destinations toward    │
│                          │ common trunks, then parallel branches), reduces redundant transfers, and avoids the       │
│                          │ exception-prone tree grafting seen in earlier generations. Edges with unknown cost (None) │
│                          │ are treated as expensive to avoid free rides on unpriced links.                           │
