│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ reuse_discounted_routing                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The current code spreads partitions across k parallel paths with a round-robin scheme,    │
│                          │ which increases total cost: later partitions are pushed onto more expensive routes even   │
│                          │ though the cost model charges for data already-delivered trunks only once. The key        │
│                          │ insight is that edge reuse should be rewarded, not penalized: once a trunk (src, dst) has │
│                          │ been paid for by an earlier destination/partition, sending subsequent partitions over it  │
│                          │ is essentially free. So we (1) drop the load-spreading round-robin and instead route      │
│                          │ every partition on its cheapest path computed with reuse-discounted edge weights, and (2) │
│                          │ add an outer refinement loop that re-runs the routing pass with `used_edges` carried over │
│                          │ from the previous pass — iterated discounting typically converges to better shared trunks │
│                          │ in 2–3 passes at trivial compute cost. This reduces redundant transfers and consolidates  │
│                          │ traffic onto already-paid routes, directly minimizing total transfer cost.                │
