│ patch_description        │ Gen 31 discounts shared trunk edges with no capacity awareness. If many destinations      │
│                          │ funnel all their partitions over one low-cost edge, aggregate demand can exceed the       │
│                          │ edge's throughput, gating the cost score despite a cheap tree. Add a hard feasibility     │
│                          │ check in `route_pass`: track how many destinations already route over each edge, and only │
│                          │ apply the reuse discount while `count` destinations' worth of flow fits within the edge's │
│                          │ `throughput` (assuming ~1 gbps per destination's partition stream as the flow unit, which │
│                          │ is the conservative per-dst demand). Once saturated, the edge reverts to its original     │
│                          │ cost so subsequent Dijkstra runs route around the bottleneck. Per Gen 27 evidence, this   │
│                          │ is applied strictly as a filter on discounting — never as active load-spreading — so the  │
│                          │ Steiner-tree behavior that drove cost down from 1035 → 744 is preserved on uncongested    │
│                          │ edges.                                                                                    │
