│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ steiner_shared_edge_broadcast                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ Both prior programs (score 0.00) route every (dst, partition) pair independently over     │
│                          │ shortest paths, paying the same expensive edges repeatedly even when many transfers share │
│                          │ a common trunk. The key insight for broadcast is that cost should be dominated by         │
│                          │ *shared* edges: a transfer along an edge already used in the same broadcast tree is       │
│                          │ redundant, so the real objective is a Steiner-tree-style structure where each dst         │
│                          │ connects to the existing tree via its cheapest attachment point. This program builds, for │
│                          │ each partition, a low-cost broadcast tree using a greedy Steiner heuristic: start from    │
│                          │ src, repeatedly attach the cheapest-to-reach remaining dst to the current tree (counting  │
│                          │ only *new* edges), and dedupe edges globally so a shared trunk edge is only transferred   │
│                          │ once. It additionally uses per-edge throughput as a feasibility filter — candidate        │
│                          │ attachments whose aggregate per-edge partition load would exceed capacity are             │
│                          │ penalized/skipped — combining the load-spreading insight from Gen 5 with the feasibility  │
│                          │ awareness of Gen 1/9 without adding gratuitous cost overhead. This exploits multi-network │
│                          │ topology by letting later dsts "ride" the already-built tree and only diverge where       │
│                          │ cheap.                                                                                    │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0130                                                                                   │
